<BaseLayout title="About" description="Learn about Shreya Roongta’s path from teaching and creating educational resources to graduate study in Learning Design and Technology."><header class="page-hero"><div class="shell narrow"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>From teaching to designing for learning</h1><p class="lede">My work is grounded in the questions educators ask every day: Who are my learners, what do they need, and how can I help them make meaning?</p></div></header><section class="section shell about-grid"><aside><p class="pull-quote">“Teaching showed me that the way a learning experience is designed can shape how someone participates, understands, and grows.”</p></aside><div class="prose"><h2>My story</h2><p>I began my career as a teacher in India and later moved to the United States. Across my experience in teaching, early childhood education, and working directly with learners, I have seen how much thoughtful planning matters. A well-designed lesson or resource can make an idea clearer, invite curiosity, and give a learner a meaningful way into the work.</p><p>Developing educational resources drew me beyond the delivery of a lesson and toward the decisions behind it. I became increasingly interested in how people learn, how curriculum creates a path through complex ideas, and how technology can strengthen an experience when it is used with care.</p><section class="journey-panel" aria-labelledby="tpt-journey"><p class="eyebrow">Teacher-created resources</p><h2 id="tpt-journey">My TPT journey</h2><p>When I moved from India to the United States, I began exploring how I could take my work as an educator beyond the classroom. As I learned to create resources for young learners, I discovered a new way to bring together my teaching experience, creativity, and understanding of how children learn.</p><p>That exploration led me to start <strong>Artful Learning Printables</strong> on Teachers Pay Teachers, where I create and sell printable and digital learning resources. My interest grew gradually with each resource I designed. The process has encouraged me to think more deeply about clear instructions, purposeful practice, visual design, and the needs of both learners and educators.</p><a class="button tpt-link" href="https://www.teacherspayteachers.com/store/artful-learning-printables">Visit Artful Learning Printables <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section><h2>Why learning design</h2><p>Learning design brings those interests together. It gives me a way to build on my experience as an educator while thinking more deeply about audience, objectives, content, interaction, and reflection. I chose graduate study in Learning Design and Technology to develop this practice with greater intention.</p><p>As I continue my studies, I am exploring curriculum development, instructional design, and educational technology. My direction is toward learning design, instructional design, or curriculum-development work in education or EdTech—work that keeps learners at the center and turns thoughtful ideas into useful experiences.</p><a class="button" href={withBase('projects/')}>See how the work develops</a></div></section></BaseLayout>
